Separate safety from progress and account for waiting (@computing-progress-assumptions: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Wait-for-a-predicate-without-losing-its-notification.lingua#r_00F33D4C3FEE0070451E2008F3") { r_00BBBFDC1DF17364A8D5372BB8
The invariant excludes a bad state. It does not make a waiting actor run. After actor 0 acquires the flag, repeatedly schedule actor 1. Each attempt sees a held flag and returns to the identical state. The resulting infinite execution never completes even though every state satisfies mutual exclusion and the counter invariant. A graph explorer that records each state once must retain this self-loop; deduplicating states is compatible with exploring reachability, but deleting the loop would erase the nonprogress execution.

Distinguish several questions. Safety asks whether every reachable state or finite history obeys a restriction. Reachability of completion asks whether some continuation can finish. Deadlock concerns participants stuck with no enabled progress under their protocol; a spinning actor can execute forever while doing no useful work, so absence of a state without outgoing edges is not a liveness proof. Starvation concerns one actor's operation indefinitely failing to complete while others may continue. Termination of this finite workload requires all actors to finish. State exactly which question a test addresses.

For this finite locked model, assume no actor fails, every critical section contains its fixed finite instructions, and scheduling is weakly fair to unfinished actors: an actor that remains eligible is eventually scheduled, and this condition applies again after each finite prefix. Every holder remains eligible and therefore executes its finite remaining instructions and releases. There are only M=Σq_i successful acquisitions available. If work remains after the last acquisition, eventually there is no holder, a fair scheduled unfinished actor acquires, contradicting that it was the last. Thus every finite job completes under these premises. This is a proof about bounded workloads and fair actor scheduling, not a universal starvation guarantee for test-and-set.

With unbounded repeated jobs, actor 0 can release and reacquire before each attempt by actor 1. Both actors can run infinitely often while actor 1 always sees a held flag. Fair scheduling of actors alone does not imply fairness of successful acquisition. A different queueing protocol, stronger scheduling conditions or a bounded workload would change the argument. If a holder crashes without releasing, the failure model changes as well; this block supplies no recovery lock.

For finite quotas, the rank R=Σ_i(5c_i+p_i) increases by one on every non-spin instruction, including release, and is unchanged on spin. Complete locked execution has exactly 5M non-spin steps, plus k failed attempts. There is no finite upper bound on k under weak fairness alone: arbitrarily many finite spins can precede a holder's next instruction. For two jobs, insert k spins after actor 0 acquires, then complete actor 0 and actor 1; total is 10+k and final x is two for every k≥0. No measured time or hardware contention cost follows without a cost model for these steps.

**Read and work:** [Operating Systems Three Easy Pieces](../sources/Operating-Systems-Three-Easy-Pieces.lingua#r_009CB72626232ED2E08E80BFC4), chapter 28's correctness, fairness and performance discussion. Reuse [Preserve process state while separating eligibility from scheduling policy](Preserve-process-state-while-separating-eligibility-from-scheduling-policy.lingua#r_009ADB7C7B16D8EF5718E860FA) to distinguish an actor's eligibility from scheduling policy. Explain why reverse graph reachability from a final state proves that every modeled state can finish, yet does not prove that every possible infinite run will finish. Construct the unfair self-loop and the unbounded-work starvation schedule in words, then identify the premise that the finite-work termination proof uses to exclude each.

